":false,"open_issues_count":3124,"license":{"key":"other","name":"Other","spdx_id":"NOASSERTION","url":null,"node_id":"MDc6TGljZW5zZTA="},"allow_forking":true,"is_template":false,"web_commit_signoff_required":false,"topics":["linter","python","typechecker","types","typing"],"visibility":"public","forks":3065,"open_issues":3124,"watchers":20064,"default_branch":"master","permissions":{"admin":false,"maintain":false,"push":false,"triage":false,"pull":true}},"pull_request":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/python/mypy/pulls/17337","html_url":"https://github.com/python/mypy/pull/17337","diff_url":"https://github.com/python/mypy/pull/17337.diff","patch_url":"https://github.com/python/mypy/pull/17337.patch","merged_at":"2024-11-14T03:58:11Z"},"body":"Fixes: #17333 \r\n\r\nThis ensures `can_be_true` and `can_be_false` on enum literals depends on the specific `Enum` fallback type behind the `Literal`, since `__bool__` can be overriden like on any other type.\r\n\r\nAdditionally typeops `true_only` and `false_only` now respect the metaclass when looking up the return values of `__bool__` and `__len__`, which ensures that a default `Enum` that doesn't override `__bool__` is still considered always truthy.\r\n","reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/python/mypy/issues/17337/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"timeline_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/python/mypy/issues/17337/timeline","performed_via_github_app":null,"state_reason":null}},"event":"cross-referenced"},{"id":15292559954,"node_id":"CE_lADOAGuhRc6LMOMjzwAAAAOPgfJS","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/python/mypy/issues/events/15292559954","actor":{"login":"hauntsaninja","id":12621235,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjEyNjIxMjM1","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/12621235?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/hauntsaninja","html_url":"https://github.com/hauntsaninja","followers_url":"https://api.github.com